# d3 — Odhrán at the ford
#
#   node tools/dialogue/compile.mjs tools/dialogue/drafts/d3.dlg
#
# ONE HELD NODE. @hold is a node directive, so it applies to every option
# in the node — there is no way to hold some options and advance on others.
# So position does no work here and notes do all of it: the hails show
# while !met_odhran, the topics show once met_odhran, sub-asks gate on the
# topic that reveals them, and the four crossings @exit.
#
# Silence is an ellipsis reply, not @silent. textPanel treats a line of
# dots as silence rather than speech, and the card holds — whereas an
# option with no reply at all skips the card and re-runs node selection,
# which can close the panel.
#
# Every line is one line with a % beneath. A block is either fully
# translated or not translated at all — mixing the two is an error.

@file d3

# ── FACTS OUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   met_odhran, knows_odhran_cannot_reckon, knows_fionnbarra,
#   knows_satire_harms, knows_seadna_name, heard_odhran_riddle,
#   knows_odhran_cave, has_toll_rope, freed_odhran
# ── FACTS IN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   has_druid_word  (from Muireann)
#
# Return visit is scene work, not dialogue: he is gone, the rope is on the
# rock as a pickup granting has_toll_rope.


= node 0 — the ford
@hold
@again [[ short line — he has not moved ]] / 

# ── the hails ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# He is on his back and does not answer the office, only the man.

* Ho, the bank!
% Hóra, a bhruach!
@when !note met_odhran
> Ho, the bank!
% Hóra, a bhruach!
< ...
% ...

* What toll is this place keeping?
% Cén dola atá á thógáil san áit seo?
@when !note met_odhran
> What toll is this place keeping?
% Cén dola atá á thógáil san áit seo?
< ...
% ...

* Are you hale? Have you taken a wound?
% An bhfuil tú slán? Ar buaileadh créacht ort?
@when !note met_odhran
@note met_odhran
> Are you hale? Have you taken a wound?
% An bhfuil tú slán? Ar buaileadh créacht ort?
< A wound, aye. That would be the word for it. I have been struck most grievously.
% Créacht, muise. Sin é an focal air. Buaileadh go trom mé.
> I see no blood.
% Ní fheicim fuil ar bith.
< Nay. I have been struck by words.
% Ní hea. Is le briathra a buaileadh mé.
< I am Odhrán, toll-keeper to Chief Fionnbarra, who holds this ford
% Is mise Odhrán, maor dola don Taoiseach Fionnbarra, ar leis an t-áth seo
< and the three crossings north to Druim Caillí.
% agus na trí áth ó thuaidh go Druim Caillí.

# ── the topics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

* Then name your toll
% Ainmnigh do dhola, mar sin
@when note met_odhran
@note knows_odhran_cannot_reckon
> Then name your toll and I will pay it or dispute it as God and custom allow.
% Ainmnigh do dhola agus íocfaidh mé é nó cuirfidh mé ina choinne, mar a cheadaíonn Dia agus nós.
< I cannot name it.
% Ní féidir liom é a ainmniú.
> Cannot?
% Ní féidir?
< Cannot. I am in woe.
% Ní féidir. Tá mé i ndólás.

* Your chief
% Do thaoiseach
@when note met_odhran
@note knows_fionnbarra
# The old toll-point question is folded in here — it is a Fionnbarra
# question, not a hail. The feast, his own good place by the fire, the
# chief speaking his name before the warriors, and the silence since.
> [[ is this the toll-point of Fionnbarra's reach? ]]
% [[ an é seo an t-áth dola atá faoi réim Fhionnbarra? ]]
< [[ it is, and these three crossings north ]]
% [[ is é, agus na trí áth seo ó thuaidh ]]
< [[ the feast, and my own good place by the fire ]]
% [[ an fhleá, agus mo dhea-áit féin cois tine ]]
< [[ he spoke my name aloud before the warriors ]]
% [[ labhair sé m'ainm os ard os comhair na laochra ]]
< [[ he has not sent for me since ]]
% [[ níor chuir sé fios orm ó shin ]]

* The one who struck you
% An té a bhuail thú
@when note met_odhran
@note knows_satire_harms
> [[ ask what was said ]]
% [[ fiafraigh cad a dúradh ]]
< [[ there was a file there — lean, sharp-tongued, clever black eyes ]]
% [[ bhí file ann — seang, géartheangach, súile dubha glice ]]
< He said that I was known from Dún Bolg to the northern stones as the man
% Dúirt sé go raibh aithne orm ó Dhún Bolg go dtí na clocha thuaidh mar an fear
< who had taken the last dry heel of bread from a widow's basket
% a thóg an tsáil dheireanach aráin thirim as ciseán baintrí
< and weighed it before returning half. He rhymed it all. It was very well made.
% agus a mheáigh í sular thug sé leath ar ais. Chuir sé i rím ar fad é. Bhí sé déanta go han-mhaith.
> That is hard.
% Is crua sin.
< He was not finished.
% Ní raibh sé críochnaithe.
< He said that I had grown so round in the belly from the taking of tolls
% Dúirt sé go raibh mé éirithe chomh cruinn sa bholg ó bheith ag tógáil dola
< that when I sat upon my rock I could not be told from it,
% nach bhféadfaí mé a aithint thar an gcarraig nuair a shuínn uirthi,
< and that the difference between Odhrán and the rock
% agus gurbh í an difríocht idir Odhrán agus an charraig
< was that the rock at least did not smack its lips.
% ná nach ndéanadh an charraig smailc lena béal, ar a laghad.

* What will you do now?
% Cad a dhéanfaidh tú anois?
@when note met_odhran
@note knows_odhran_cave
> [[ ask what becomes of him ]]
% [[ fiafraigh cad atá i ndán dó ]]
< I heard there is a cave above Cnoc Maol
% Chuala mé go bhfuil uaimh os cionn Chnoc Maol
< where a man might sit very quietly
% mar a bhféadfadh fear suí go han-chiúin
< and not collect tolls from anyone ever again.
% gan dola a bhailiú ó dhuine ar bith go deo arís.
< I could leave my toll-rope and go in with the monks.
% D'fhéadfainn mo théad dola a fhágáil agus dul isteach leis na manaigh.
< They sit in the dark and they reckon nothing,
% Suíonn siad sa dorchadas agus ní áiríonn siad faic,
< and no one mocks them for it. It is considered holy.
% agus ní dhéanann duine ar bith fonóid fúthu dá bharr. Meastar gur rud naofa é.

# ── the sub-asks ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

* Has he a name?
% An bhfuil ainm air?
@when note knows_satire_harms
@note knows_seadna_name
> Has he a name?
% An bhfuil ainm air?
< Séadna.
% Séadna.
< [[ and where he is now, or where he was going ]]
% [[ agus cá bhfuil sé anois, nó cá raibh a thriall ]]

* Was that the worst of it?
% An é sin an chuid ba mheasa?
@when note knows_satire_harms
@note heard_odhran_riddle
> Was that the worst of it?
% An é sin an chuid ba mheasa?
< That was not the worst. The worst was the riddle he bound into his mocking.
% Níorbh é sin an chuid ba mheasa. Ba é an tomhas a cheangail sé isteach ina aor an chuid ba mheasa.
< He turned upon me his full craft, before all, and he spoke thus:
% Dhírigh sé a cheird ar fad orm, os comhair cách, agus labhair sé mar seo:
< "Odhrán! Odhrán! Thou great and mighty counter of small coins!
% "A Odhráin! A Odhráin! A áiritheoir mhóir na mbonn beag!
< Thou shalt have no peace, until thou hast reckoned truly this:
% Ní bheidh suaimhneas agat go dtí go mbeidh seo áirithe go fírinneach agat:
< The number of thy years divided by the number of honest men thou hast known.
% Líon do bhlianta roinnte ar líon na bhfear ionraic ar chuir tú aithne orthu.
< Add what the dead owe the living,
% Cuir leis an méid atá ag na mairbh ar na beo,
< less the worth of mercy shown but never asked for.
% lúide luach na trócaire a tugadh gan iarraidh.
< Multiply what the wren sings at morning
% Iolraigh a gcanann an dreoilín ar maidin
< by the silence of the stone at midnight.
% faoi thost na cloiche i lár na hoíche.
< Pay this to the man who wronged thee.
% Íoc é seo leis an té a rinne éagóir ort.
< Keep nothing. Owe nothing. Be free."
% Ná coinnigh faic. Ná bíodh fiacha ort. Bí saor."
< I do not know what a dead man owes the living. I do not know how to subtract mercy.
% Níl a fhios agam cad atá ag fear marbh ar na beo. Níl a fhios agam conas trócaire a dhealú.
< I cannot find the man I wronged. I cannot find the man who wronged me.
% Ní féidir liom an té a ndearna mé éagóir air a aimsiú. Ní féidir liom an té a rinne éagóir orm a aimsiú.
< And since that night I cannot reckon.
% Agus ón oíche sin i leith ní thig liom áireamh a dhéanamh.

* Your rope, then
% Do théad, mar sin
@when note knows_odhran_cave
@note has_toll_rope
> [[ ask for the rope ]]
% [[ iarr an téad ]]
< I did stretch it across the ford when I had traffic.
% Shín mé trasna an átha í nuair a bhí trácht agam.
< It is a good rope. Take it. Take it freely. No toll.
% Is maith an téad í. Tóg leat í. Tóg saor in aisce í. Gan dola.

# ── the crossings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All four @exit. Crossing ends the conversation, which is what crossing
# should do. Boast leaves him exactly as he was; custom passes you free by
# failing; the druid word passes you as one under protection; only the
# riddle changes anything for him.

* [[ boast label ]]
@when note met_odhran
@exit
> [[ you are who you are, and the rope is a rope ]]
< [[ he stands aside — not moved, only outmatched ]]

* [[ custom label ]]
@when note met_odhran
@exit
> [[ then I have named it and you have not, so I pass ]]
< [[ he cannot answer it, and does not enjoy losing this way ]]

* [[ druid word label ]]
@when note has_druid_word
@exit
> [[ Muireann's word ]]
< [[ pass, as one under protection ]]
< [[ but what did it mean? ]]

* [[ riddle answer label ]]
@when note heard_odhran_riddle
@note freed_odhran
@exit
> [[ the sum is nothing — keep nothing, owe nothing, be free ]]
< [[ he does not thank you. he sits up. ]]

# ── always ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

* Walk on
% Siúil leat
@exit
@silent
